+| `tsconfig.json` | Excludes `supabase/functions` (Deno, not browser) |
+
+---
+
+## D. Schema changes
+
+### Reused, not recreated
+
+`conversations`, `conversation_members`, `messages`, `profiles`,
+`storage.objects`. No duplicate tables were introduced.
+
+### Added
+
+| Object | Table | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `client_id` + unique index `(sender_id, client_id)` | `messages` | Makes a retried send idempotent |
+| `conversation_reads` | new | Read watermark per member per conversation |
+| `default_ttl_seconds` | `conversations` | Persists the disappearing-message setting |
+| `calls`, `call_signals` | new | Durable, authorised signalling |
+| `albums`, `album_items` | new | Timed albums |
+| `app_secrets` | new | Holds the TURN shared secret, unreachable via the API |
+| `push_subscriptions` | new | One row per browser install |
+| `album-media` bucket | `storage.buckets` | Private, 10 MB, image/video MIME only |
+
+### Indexes added
+
+`messages(conversation_id, created_at desc)`,
+`messages(expires_at) where not null`, `calls_one_live_per_conversation`
+(partial unique), `calls(caller_id, created_at desc)`, `calls(callee_id, …)`,
+`call_signals(call_id, id)`, `albums(owner_id)`, `albums(conversation_id)`,
+`albums(expires_at)`, `album_items(album_id, sort_order)`,
+`conversation_reads(user_id, last_read_at desc)`,
+`push_subscriptions(user_id) where disabled_at is null`.
+
+### Data preservation
+
+Every statement is `add column if not exists` / `create table if not exists` /
+`create index if not exists` / `drop policy if exists` followed by a `create`.
+No `drop table`, no `drop column`, no `delete` outside the purge routines.
+Existing rows get `client_id = null` (allowed by the partial index) and
+`default_ttl_seconds = 0` (auto-delete off — the same behaviour they have today).
+Rollback notes are at the foot of each migration.
+
+---
+
+## E–H. Implementation summary
+
+### E. Turning tracks into a settled connection
+
+`CONNECTED` now comes only from `RTCPeerConnection.connectionState`. `ontrack`
+attaches the stream and says nothing about state. `disconnected` becomes
+`RECONNECTING` with an 8-second grace period before recovery is attempted, and
+only a bounded number of ICE restarts (3) is tried before the call is honestly
+reported as failed. Only the caller renegotiates, so two peers cannot both offer.
+
+### F. What actually made calls ring
+
+Two changes, in order of impact:
+
+1. **The invitation is a row.** `calls` is inserted before the callee is
+   contacted. If their socket is down, the row waits. On reconnect,
+   `postgres_changes` delivers it, or `surfaceIncoming` finds it — and either
+   way the caller's phone is still ringing because the caller never depended on
+   the callee's socket in the first place.
+2. **Signals are drained, not just subscribed to.** A client joining a call
+   reads every `call_signals` row above `lastAppliedSignalId` and applies them in
+   order. A missed message delays the call by one drain interval (2.5 s) instead
+   of losing it.
+
+ICE candidates that arrive before their description are buffered rather than
+discarded. This was a silent cause of connections that never formed.
+
+### G. Recovery, teardown and cleanup
+
+* Heartbeats every 15 s while a call is live, plus `expire_stale_calls()` every
+  minute: a peer who force-quits becomes `ENDED — connection lost` within 45 s
+  instead of staying "connected" forever.
+* `pagehide` no longer tears a call down. On iOS that fires whenever the user
+  glances at another app, and the old behaviour dropped healthy calls. Only a
+  genuine unload (not a back/forward-cache restore) ends the call.
+* On returning to the foreground, signals are re-drained.
+* Teardown detaches every handler before closing the peer connection, so the
+  close event cannot re-enter the state machine, then stops all local and remote
+  tracks, releases both channels, and clears every timer.
+
+### H. Notifications and push
+
+Three surfaces, chosen by state rather than by configuration:
+
+| Situation | Surface |
+|---|---|
+| App open, conversation on screen | **No notification.** The message is already visible. |
+| App open, elsewhere | In-app toast, tappable, naming the sender |
+| App hidden, push available | System notification via the service worker |
+| App hidden, push unavailable | Nothing until the app is reopened — and the notification settings screen says why |
+
+The capability detector reports the truth on iOS: Web Push exists only in a
+Home-Screen web app from 16.4 onwards. It never reports push as available in a
+plain Safari tab, and the UI shows an "add to Home Screen" explanation instead of
+a dead button.
+
+Push payloads carry **no message content**. Message bodies are encrypted
+client-side and the server cannot read them, so a push says *who* and *that there
+is a message*, and marks expiring ones with a time note. Pretending otherwise
+would require the server to read plaintext.
+
+### I. Ephemeral messages
+
+`expires_at` is set by the sender, stored on the row, and enforced in three
+independent places:
+
+1. **Reads** — `messages_not_expired_read`, a RESTRICTIVE policy, so it ANDs
+   with whatever permissive policies exist and can only narrow access. Expired
+   content is unreachable through the API, including by its author.
+2. **Purge** — `purge_expired_messages()`, scheduled with `pg_cron` where
+   available and documented for external scheduling where not.
+3. **Realtime removal** — UPDATE and DELETE events remove the bubble without a
+   refresh.
+
+`client_id` plus a unique index `(sender_id, client_id)` makes a retried send
+idempotent, so a timeout cannot produce two messages.
+
+The UI countdown renders `expires_at`; it never creates it. A tab left open for
+a week shows nothing that has expired, because the server would not return it.
+
+### J. Timed albums
+
+`albums.expires_at` is the authority, with 1 h / 6 h / 24 h / 7 d and a custom
+duration. Access is decided by one function, `gayze_can_view_album()`, which
+checks expiry **before** ownership — an expired album is expired for its owner
+too. The Storage read policy calls that same function, so no signed URL can be
+minted for an expired album's object, and existing signatures die within five
+minutes.
+
+Path shape is enforced by a CHECK constraint:
+`{owner_id}/{album_id}/{item_id}.{ext}`. A row can never point outside its
+owner's folder, which is what stops one member referencing another's object.
+
+**Encryption, stated plainly:** conversation albums are encrypted with the
+existing per-conversation AES-GCM key before upload, so the backend holds
+ciphertext. **Private albums are not end-to-end encrypted** — a personal album
+has no conversation key to encrypt against, so it relies on the private bucket,
+RLS and short-lived signed URLs. The UI does not claim otherwise.
+
+### K. Security and RLS
+
+* **No policy was replaced.** The live permissive policies are not in this
+  repository, and guessing their names to redefine them would be reckless.
+  Everything added is either a new table's own policy or a **RESTRICTIVE**
+  policy, which can only ever narrow access. A policy is never dropped except by
+  name where this repo created it.
+* **RLS is never disabled**, and `security.test.ts` fails the build if a future
+  migration contains `disable row level security`, creates a table without
+  enabling RLS, or grants a definer function to `anon`.
+* **Authorisation, not obscurity.** Knowing a conversation id is not sufficient
+  to insert a message, open a call, or append a signal: each policy requires
+  `auth.uid()` to be a member of that conversation or a participant of that call.
+* **`app_secrets`** has RLS enabled with **no policies at all**, so neither
+  `anon` nor `authenticated` can read it through the API. Only the
+  `SECURITY DEFINER` function can.
+* **The push sender cannot read messages.** `push_targets_for_message()` returns
+  identity and delivery fields only — no ciphertext, no nonce, no key.
+* **TURN credentials** are short-lived HMACs minted by
+  `get_turn_credentials()`; the shared secret never leaves the database. The
+  client-source test fails if `VITE_TURN_*` returns.
+* **Storage** policies are scoped to the owner's path segment and call the album
+  access function for reads.
+
+### L. Tests
+
+`npm test` — 43 tests, all passing. `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint` and
+`npm run build` are green.
+
+| Suite | Tests | Covers |
+|---|---|---|
