+## B. FIXED IN THIS PASS
+
+Ordered by severity. Each was proven by execution before and after, not inferred.
+
+### B1 — A sender could un-disappear their own messages — **FIXED**
+
+RLS constrains *which rows* you may touch, never *what they become*: a policy
+expression sees only the new row, so it cannot express "this column must not
+change". The only `UPDATE` policy on `messages` checked `sender_id = auth.uid()`,
+which constrains the author and nothing else.
+
+Proven by probe: a sender could set `expires_at = null` and make a disappearing
+message permanent, or push it ten years out. The disappearing-message promise is
+made to the **recipient** — someone who read "disappears in 10 seconds" and
+relied on it had no guarantee at all.
+
+Fixed with a `BEFORE UPDATE` trigger guard on `messages`, `albums` and
+`album_items`. A trigger, rather than column privileges, because PostgreSQL
+documentation is explicit that column-level privileges cannot narrow a
+table-level `UPDATE` grant, and Supabase grants broadly. The guard applies to
+client roles only, so maintenance can still repair data.
+
+### B2 — Burning a message was impossible — **FIXED**
+
+The more interesting of the two, and found only because a lifecycle test failed
+in a way that did not match its own expectation.
+
+`messages_not_expired_read` excludes burned rows — correct, since a burned
+message must be unreadable by everyone including its sender. But PostgreSQL
+validates the **new row of an `UPDATE`** against the SELECT policies as well as
+the UPDATE policy's `WITH CHECK`. So setting `burned_at` made the new row fail
+the SELECT policy, the update was rejected, and the row was left untouched — with
+or without `RETURNING`. Same shape as the album defect: a policy that correctly
+hides a row also prevents that row from being written.
+
+Fixed with `burn_message(uuid)`, SECURITY DEFINER, since a function is the only
+way to set a column whose new value is deliberately unreadable. The privilege
+that buys is paid for explicitly: it refuses a caller with no JWT, and returns
+`false` for a message that is not the caller's **exactly** as it does for one that
+does not exist, so it cannot be used to probe whether an id is real. Eight
+behaviours verified, including idempotency and `anon` denial.
+
+**Not user-facing today:** the client primitive had no caller. That makes it
+latent rather than live — and the reason it is worth fixing is that the next
+person to wire up the button would have found it silently did nothing.
+
+### B3 — A signed URL could outlive its album, and the comment claimed otherwise — **FIXED**
+
+Storage signatures cannot be revoked: once issued, a URL is valid for the whole
+TTL it was minted with, whatever happens to the album. A fixed five-minute TTL
+therefore meant a URL minted four minutes before expiry stayed valid for four
+minutes after the album was gone. The brief's requirement — that an expired album
+must not be reachable because someone obtained a URL earlier — was not satisfied.
+
+The signature is now never given more time than the album has left
+(`min(300s, remaining)`), and an already-expired album is not signed at all.
+Checked as a property across the whole range rather than at chosen points.
+
+### B4 — Chat images could produce a message that silently never arrived — **FIXED**
+
+The chat path did no preparation: a 5 MB file became a ~6.7 MB base64 string
+inside the encrypted message body. A body that size is unlikely to fit the
+Realtime event carrying it, so the recipient would not see the message until
+something forced a refetch — a send that appears to succeed and does not arrive.
+
+Attachments are now downscaled to a 2048px long edge and re-encoded before
+encryption, with an explicit ceiling that fails **loudly** rather than sending
+something that may not be delivered. The encoder is shared with the profile-photo
+path rather than duplicated, and returns the original on any failure, so nothing
+new can break a send. The profile path keeps its own 1600px policy unchanged.
+
+### B5 — The disappearing-message picker could not produce the specified lifetimes — **FIXED**
+
+The database accepted 0/10/60/300/3600/86400/604800 but the picker offered only
+0/5m/1h/24h, so the product could not create the 10-second, 1-minute or 7-day
+messages the specification names. Now offered, with values and labels derived
+from one table instead of an array plus a separate chain of `if`s — which is how
+a value could previously be added and still render as a raw number of seconds.
+
+### B6 — Push delivery outcomes were untested — **FIXED**
+
+The distinction between "this endpoint is gone" and "our key is wrong" lived in
+Deno code outside the typechecker and outside any test. The case that matters is
+401: a wrong VAPID key returns 401 for every device at once, so retiring
+subscriptions on it would delete the entire recipient list in one pass and turn a
+broken deploy into unrecoverable data loss. Now an explicit, tested function,
+checked as a property across all 500 status codes.
+
+### B7 — Test and diagnostic robustness — **FIXED**
+
+* The migration-count assertion was hard-coded, so adding a migration produced a
+  failure that said nothing. It now compares files on disk against files applied,
+  which actually catches a migration left unapplied.
+* `expectDenied` recognised only a denial's prose. It now honours the SQLSTATE
+  (`42501`), so a guard is recognised as a refusal however its message is worded.
+* `docs/PREFLIGHT.sql` had a real defect — `aclexplode.grantee` is an `oid` and
+  needs joining to role names — plus a missing migration and no check that the
+  lifetime guards are enabled.
