+**The Storage *API* was not exercised.** No live project, so no bucket, no
+upload, no signed URL, no expiry.
+
+What *is* verified, against real PostgreSQL:
+
+- the bucket is declared private and the policies are scoped by ownership;
+- an expired album's object is **not selectable**, which is the precondition for
+  no signature being mintable;
+- album item paths are constrained so that `{owner_id}/{album_id}/{item_id}.{ext}`
+  is derivable from the row — a member cannot record a path into someone else's
+  folder;
+- the client caps a signature's TTL by the album's remaining lifetime.
+
+**The exact limitation, stated plainly:** a Supabase Storage signed URL **cannot
+be revoked**. Once Storage issues one, it is valid for the whole TTL it was
+minted with, regardless of anything that happens to the album afterwards.
+
+This is why server-side expiry alone does **not** satisfy the brief's
+requirement. The guarantee is provided by two mechanisms together:
+
+1. at signing time, Storage re-evaluates the access policy — no new signature
+   after expiry;
+2. the client never requests a signature longer than the album has left, so an
+   already-issued URL dies no later than the album does.
+
+Mechanism 2 is a **client-side** guarantee. It holds only while the signing code
+is the code in this repository; any other client — or a hand-crafted request —
+can ask for a longer TTL while the album is still live, and that URL will
+outlive the album.
+
+**Closing this properly requires either a Storage-side TTL cap or moving media
+behind an application-controlled endpoint.** That is an architecture change and
+is not attempted here.
+
+---
+
+## TURN
