+# MESSAGING — where it breaks, and what to run to find out
+
+**Status: the failure is NOT yet identified, because this environment cannot see
+the live system.** No `.env`, no Supabase CLI, no `psql`, no credentials, no
+project URL anywhere in the repository, and no browser or device. Every layer
+that needs the live project is therefore reported **UNVERIFIED**, and nothing
+below claims otherwise.
+
+What follows is the exact instrument needed to identify the break in one pass,
+the runbook for interpreting it, and the one hypothesis that fits the symptom
+best — with the check that confirms or kills it in about sixty seconds.
+
+---
+
+## The leading hypothesis, and the sixty-second check
+
+`IS_LIVE_BACKEND` is defined as `isSupabaseConfigured`, which is
+`Boolean(VITE_SUPABASE_URL && VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY)` — evaluated
+**when the bundle is built**, because Vite inlines `import.meta.env` at build
+time.
+
+If either variable was absent during the Cloudflare build, the whole app runs in
+demo mode. That produces **exactly the reported symptom**:
+
+- you type a message, and it appears in your own thread;
+- it is never inserted into the database;
+- the other person receives nothing;
+- **nothing errors**, because demo mode is a legitimate state rather than a
+  failure — no toast, no console error, no failed request.
+
+**Check it in one line.** Replace the host with your deployment:
+
+```bash
+curl -s https://YOUR-DOMAIN/ | grep -oE '/assets/index-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\.js' | head -1 | \
+  xargs -I{} curl -s https://YOUR-DOMAIN{} | grep -oE 'https://[a-z0-9]{20}\.supabase\.co' | head -1
+```
+
+A project URL means credentials were baked in. **No output means `VITE_SUPABASE_URL`
+was not set at build time and that is the bug** — set both variables in
+Cloudflare Pages → Settings → Environment variables and **redeploy**; changing
+them without a new build changes nothing, because they are inlined at build time.
+
+> An important limit on this check: a build produced *inside this sandbox* also
+> contains no Supabase URL, because no credentials exist here. That is expected
+> and says nothing whatsoever about your deployment. Only the check above, run
+> against the real domain, is evidence.
+
+---
+
+## Second hypothesis — a conversation that has no second member
+
+Nothing in this repository, and no migration, creates rows in `conversations` or
+`conversation_members`. The app only ever **reads** them. If the production
+trigger that creates a conversation on a mutual match is missing or has never
+fired for your two accounts, there is nothing to send into and no conversation
+appears in the list.
+
+The subtle variant is worse: a conversation with **one** member appears in the
+list, so the UI looks correct, but every INSERT is refused by the membership
+policy. `?diag=1` reports this case explicitly as "the other person is NOT a
+member".
+
+---
+
+## Third hypothesis — the peer key was never published, and the app blames the contact
+
+This one came out of tracing the send path and is worth knowing before you start,
+because it produces a **confidently wrong message**.
+
+On boot the app registers this device's public key by calling
+`register_identity_device` — inside a `try`/`catch` that reduces every failure to
+`console.warn`. If that function is missing from the project, or denies the call,
+the device is never registered. Then `get_conversation_peer_key` returns `NULL`
+for anyone who opens a chat with you, and `resolveDirectKey` returns:
+
+> "This contact has not published a device key yet, so the chat cannot be encrypted."
+
+The message blames your contact. The actual cause is an upstream function that
+does not exist or refuses to run — and nothing anywhere reported it.
+
+`loadConversationPeerKey` swallows every error the same way, so an RPC that does
+not exist, an RLS denial and a genuinely unpublished key are **indistinguishable**
+from the user's side. The `DEVICE IDENTITY` and `ENCRYPTION` checks in the
+diagnostics separate all three by reporting the raw error code.
+
+---
+
+## Layer-by-layer status
+
+`STATIC` is what the code path does as written, verified by reading it. `LIVE` is
+what the running system does, which needs the project. They are not the same
+claim, and only the second one answers "does messaging work".
+
+| Layer | STATIC (code as written) | LIVE (required evidence) |
+|---|---|---|
+| **MESSAGE SEND** | PASS — path is complete: key resolve → encrypt → optimistic `SENDING` bubble → persist → reconcile → `FAILED` + toast on throw. But it is gated on `IS_LIVE_BACKEND` at `App.tsx:151`, and `isSupabaseRoom` additionally requires a UUID-shaped room id | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **DATABASE INSERT** | PASS — inserts the encrypted row, retries without `client_id` if the schema rejects it | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **DATABASE SELECT** | PASS — reads membership, then messages, with expiry predicates | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **RLS** | PASS for the policies this repository defines (144 tests against real PostgreSQL). **The production policies are not readable from here** | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **REALTIME SUBSCRIPTION** | PASS — one inbox channel per user (`gayze-inbox-${userId}`), ref-counted, released on unmount. Cannot be confirmed connected from the channel object existing | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **REALTIME EVENT** | PASS — INSERT handled with a duplicate guard and a race-safe reconcile | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **DECRYPTION** | PASS — AES-GCM, hex-encoded body, per-conversation key from the peer's published JWK | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **REACT STATE** | PASS — the optimistic bubble is dropped when the stored row arrives, so no duplicate | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **MESSAGE RENDER** | PASS — renders from state with no second network path | **UNVERIFIED** |
+| **CLOUDFLARE DEPLOYMENT** | Cannot be compared from here | **MATCH/MISMATCH: UNVERIFIED** |
+| **SUPABASE PROJECT** | Cannot be reached from here | **MATCH/MISMATCH: UNVERIFIED** |
+
+The nine `UNVERIFIED` rows are not hedging. "Messages do not work" and "the send
+path is written correctly" are both true at once, and the only layer that can
+explain that is the one no test in this repository touches: the live integration.
+
+---
+
+## What I built to close the gap
+
+**`?diag=1` on the deployed URL.** Append it to your production address and the
+panel walks the pipeline in the order a message travels, stopping at the first
+failure and printing the raw error:
+
+```
+ENVIRONMENT → AUTH → DEVICE IDENTITY → MEMBERSHIP → ENCRYPTION
+  → DATABASE INSERT → DATABASE SELECT → RLS → REALTIME
+```
+
+It is reachable in production deliberately — a diagnostic that only works locally
+cannot diagnose a deployment. It performs a real INSERT as part of the check
+(two-minute lifetime, deleted immediately), and the realtime probe records
+**every** status transition rather than trusting that a channel object exists.
+
+It prints the project reference and the build stamp. It never prints a key, a
+token, a JWT or any message content.
+
+**`docs/MESSAGING_RUNBOOK.md`** is the operator's half: the demo-mode check, the
+membership SQL that catches a one-member conversation, the policies-versus-grants
+distinction that explains a `42501`, the realtime publication and replica-identity
+queries, and a single closing query that settles the database questions at once.
+Everything in it is read-only apart from two `alter` statements that are labelled
+as writes.
+
+---
+
+## About the tests
+
+There are 252 tests and 144 of them execute real PostgreSQL. **None of that is
+evidence that messaging works in the deployed app**, and it should not be cited as
+such. It is evidence about the database layer and the pure logic above it. The
+break being chased here lives in the layer those tests cannot reach, which is
+precisely why it survived a green suite.
+
+---
+
+## The fastest path to an answer
+
+1. Run the `curl` check at the top. If it prints nothing, **stop** — you have the
+   answer, and it is a Cloudflare environment variable plus a redeploy.
+2. Otherwise open `?diag=1` on the deployed URL. Read the first red card.
+3. If it stops at MEMBERSHIP, run the membership SQL in the runbook — the
+   production-only conversation-creation trigger is the prime suspect for "we
+   match but have no conversation".
+4. If it stops at DEVICE IDENTITY or ENCRYPTION, send me the raw error code in
+   that card. `42883` / `PGRST202` means the function does not exist in the
+   project; a returned `NULL` means the key was never published; `42501` means a
+   grant or policy.
+
+Send me the panel's output — or the first failing card and its error code — and I
+can name the fix exactly. I will not guess at it from here.
