+# MESSAGING — diagnosis runbook
+
+**For: "messages do not work in the deployed app."**
+
+Work top to bottom. Each step isolates one layer. **Stop at the first FAIL** —
+everything below it may be failing only because that one did, and fixing layers
+out of order wastes the afternoon.
+
+Two tools, both already in the repository:
+
+| Tool | Where | What it answers |
+|---|---|---|
+| `?diag=1` | Append to your deployed URL, e.g. `https://gayze.app/?diag=1` | Which layer fails, live, with your session |
+| This file | SQL editor + Cloudflare dashboard | What the database and the build actually contain |
+
+The diagnostics panel is reachable in production on purpose. A diagnostic that
+only works locally cannot diagnose a deployment. It writes one row as part of the
+INSERT probe, carries a two-minute lifetime, and deletes it immediately.
+
+---
+
+## Step 0 — Is the deployed app even connected? (Do this first.)
+
+**The single most likely cause, and it produces no error at all.**
+
+`IS_LIVE_BACKEND` is `Boolean(VITE_SUPABASE_URL && VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY)`,
+evaluated **at build time**. If either is missing when Cloudflare builds, the
+entire app runs in demo mode: your own messages appear in your own thread, they
+are never sent, the other person receives nothing, and nothing fails, because
+demo mode is a legitimate state rather than an error.
+
+**Check it in one command.** Replace the URL with your deployment:
+
+```bash
+# Does the deployed bundle contain your Supabase project URL?
+curl -s https://YOUR-DOMAIN/ | grep -oE '/assets/index-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\.js' | head -1 | \
+  xargs -I{} curl -s https://YOUR-DOMAIN{} | grep -oE 'https://[a-z0-9]{20}\.supabase\.co' | head -1
+```
+
+- **Prints a project URL** → credentials were baked in. Go to Step 1.
+- **Prints nothing** → `VITE_SUPABASE_URL` was **not** set at build time. **This
+  is your bug.** Set both variables in Cloudflare Pages → Settings → Environment
+  variables, then **redeploy** — Vite inlines them at build time, so changing
+  them without a new build changes nothing.
+
+Or open `https://YOUR-DOMAIN/?diag=1` and read the first card. It states which
+mode you are in and names both variables.
+
+```
+VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
+VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=<publishable / anon key>
+VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=<optional, for push>
+```
+
+**Also confirm the deployment is the code you think it is.** The panel prints a
+build stamp (`ISO timestamp + commit sha`) injected at build time. If it is not
+the commit you expected, the deployment is stale and every other investigation is
+against the wrong code.
+
+---
+
+## Step 1 — Are you both members of the same conversation?
+
+A conversation and its membership rows are created outside this application — in
+the production project, presumably by a trigger on mutual interest. **Nothing in
+this repository creates them**, which is why this step comes early: the app can
+only read them.
+
+Run in the SQL editor. Substitute the two user ids (`?diag=1` shows yours):
+
+```sql
+-- Do the two accounts share a conversation, and is each a member?
+with pair as (
+  select unnest(array['<USER_A_UUID>','<USER_B_UUID>']::uuid[]) as user_id
+)
+select
+  cm.conversation_id,
+  count(*)                                   as member_count,
+  bool_and(cm.user_id = any (array['<USER_A_UUID>','<USER_B_UUID>']::uuid[])) as only_these_two,
+  array_agg(cm.user_id order by cm.user_id)  as member_ids
+from public.conversation_members cm
+where cm.conversation_id in (
+  select conversation_id from public.conversation_members
+  where user_id = '<USER_A_UUID>'
+  intersect
+  select conversation_id from public.conversation_members
+  where user_id = '<USER_B_UUID>'
+)
+group by cm.conversation_id;
+```
+
+| Result | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| **A row, `member_count = 2`** | Membership is correct. Go to Step 2. |
+| **No rows** | You share no conversation. The chat list will be empty, and a send has nowhere to go. The conversation-creation trigger is not firing — or you are not matched. |
+| **A row with `member_count = 1`** | **The subtle one.** The conversation appears in the list, so the UI looks right, but an INSERT is refused because the other person is not a member. This is exactly the case `?diag=1` flags with "the other person is NOT a member". |
+
+Confirm the tables the app reads are the tables that exist:
+
+```sql
+select table_name from information_schema.tables
+where table_schema = 'public'
+  and table_name in ('conversations','conversation_members','messages','conversation_reads')
+order by table_name;
+```
+
+If `conversation_members` does not exist, the production project uses a different
+name — and **every membership check in this repository is against the wrong
+table**, which alone would explain messaging failing while the tests pass. Tell
+me the real name and I will re-point the policies.
+
+---
+
+## Step 2 — Can the database accept a row at all?
+
+Run as **User A** (the SQL editor runs as superuser, which bypasses RLS — so this
+only proves the schema, not the policy). `?diag=1` performs the equivalent test
+**through the real API with the real session**, which is the one that matters.
+
+```sql
+-- Schema sanity: does messages have every column the client writes?
+select column_name, data_type, is_nullable, column_default
+from information_schema.columns
+where table_schema = 'public' and table_name = 'messages'
+order by ordinal_position;
+```
+
+The client writes `conversation_id, sender_id, ciphertext, nonce, expires_at` and
+optionally `client_id`. **A missing `client_id` is survivable** — the client
+detects the schema error and retries without it — but missing `expires_at` or
+`nonce` is not, because the restrictive policies reference them.
+
+Now the policies:
+
+```sql
+select policyname, permissive, cmd, roles::text, coalesce(qual, with_check) as predicate
+from pg_policies
+where schemaname = 'public' and tablename = 'messages'
+order by cmd, permissive desc, policyname;
+```
+
+**Read this carefully.** PostgreSQL combines policies as:
+
+```
+(any PERMISSIVE policy matches)  AND  (all RESTRICTIVE policies match)
+```
+
+so a table with RLS and **no PERMISSIVE policy for INSERT is denied for
+everyone**, and no restrictive policy can grant it back. If there is no row with
+`permissive = 'PERMISSIVE'` and `cmd = 'INSERT'`, that is your bug.
+
+Then the grants — a missing grant fails **before** any policy is consulted, and
+the error says "permission denied for table" rather than "row-level security":
+
+```sql
+select
+  has_table_privilege('authenticated','public.messages','SELECT') as auth_select,
+  has_table_privilege('authenticated','public.messages','INSERT') as auth_insert,
+  has_table_privilege('authenticated','public.messages','UPDATE') as auth_update,
+  has_table_privilege('authenticated','public.messages','DELETE') as auth_delete;
+```
+
+All four must be `true`.
+
+---
+
+## Step 3 — Does an INSERT actually land?
+
+`?diag=1` does this for you: it inserts a real row as you, reads it back, lists
+the conversation to confirm the row is visible, then deletes it. It reports the
+raw error code.
+
+If you would rather do it by hand, open the browser console on the deployed app,
+signed in, and paste:
+
+```js
+// Replace with a conversation id from the membership query in Step 1.
+const conversationId = '<CONVERSATION_UUID>';
+const { data: s } = await window.__gayze.supabase.auth.getSession();
+const { data, error } = await window.__gayze.supabase
+  .from('messages')
+  .insert({
+    conversation_id: conversationId,
+    sender_id: s.session.user.id,
+    ciphertext: '__manual_probe__',
+    nonce: null,
+    expires_at: new Date(Date.now() + 120000).toISOString(),
+  })
+  .select('id')
+  .single();
+console.log({ data, error });
+```
+
+| Error code | Meaning | Fix |
+|---|---|---|
+| `42501` + "row-level security" | The membership check failed | Step 1 — confirm **both** of you are members |
+| `42501` + "permission denied for table" | No GRANT for `authenticated` | Apply `20260928130000_messaging_reliability.sql` |
+| `42703` | A column does not exist | Step 2 — the live schema differs from the client |
+| `23503` | Foreign key failed | The conversation or profile does not exist |
+| `PGRST301` / 401 | Session invalid | Sign out and back in |
+
+**If the INSERT succeeds**, the send path and the policies are fine, and the
+problem is downstream. Go to Step 4. Do not change the send path.
+
+---
+
+## Step 4 — Does Realtime deliver the event?
+
+`?diag=1` opens a raw `postgres_changes` subscription and records **every** status
+the channel reports. A channel object existing proves nothing: Supabase reports
+`CHANNEL_ERROR` and `TIMED_OUT` on the same object it reports `SUBSCRIBED` on.
+
+Expected: `SUBSCRIBING → SUBSCRIBED`.
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `CHANNEL_ERROR` | The subscription was refused — usually RLS or a missing grant |
+| `TIMED_OUT` | The socket connected but the channel never joined |
+| `CLOSED` | Something tore the channel down |
+
+Then, **with the panel open**, send a message from the other account. The
+REALTIME section should show an INSERT event within a second.
+
+- **Event appears** → transport works. The break is decryption or rendering.
+- **No event, status SUBSCRIBED** → check the publication:
+
+```sql
+select tablename from pg_publication_tables
+where pubname = 'supabase_realtime' and schemaname = 'public'
+order by tablename;
+```
+
+`messages` must be present. If it is not:
+
+```sql
+alter publication supabase_realtime add table public.messages;
+```
+
+Also confirm replica identity — without `FULL`, an UPDATE or DELETE arrives
+without the previous row, so an expired or burned bubble cannot be removed:
+
+```sql
+select c.relname, c.relreplident
+from pg_class c join pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace
+where n.nspname = 'public' and c.relname in ('messages','albums','album_items');
+
+-- 'f' is FULL. If it shows 'd', run:
+-- alter table public.messages replica identity full;
+```
+
+---
+
+## Step 5 — Decryption
+
+If a row is stored and the event arrives but the message never renders, the
+remaining candidates are the encryption key and the rendering path.
+
+**A message that is stored but cannot be decrypted is not a transport problem** —
+do not touch the send path.
+
+The direct (one-to-one) key is derived from the peer's published device key. If
+that key has never been published, the send is refused before it reaches the
+database, and inbound messages render as `[Encrypted message]`.
+
+`?diag=1` calls `get_conversation_peer_key` and reports whether it returned a
+key. To check by hand:
+
+```sql
+-- As the authenticated user, through the API — not the SQL editor:
+select public.get_conversation_peer_key('<CONVERSATION_UUID>');
+```
+
+- **Returns a key** → the send path can encrypt. If messages still do not render,
+  the problem is decrypting: the two devices derived different keys.
+- **Returns NULL** → the other person has not published a device key. Have them
+  sign in and open the app, which publishes it, then retry.
+
+Check whether the identity tables exist at all:
+
+```sql
+select table_name from information_schema.tables
+where table_schema = 'public'
+  and table_name in ('identity_devices','conversation_key_envelopes','device_identities')
+order by table_name;
+```
+
+`?diag=1` reports the exact error if this call fails.
+
+---
+
+## Step 6 — Is the deployed code the current code?
+
+The panel prints a build stamp. Compare it with:
+
+```bash
+git log -1 --format='%H %cI'
+```
+
+If the deployed stamp is older than the commit you are testing, **every other
+finding is against the wrong code**. Redeploy and re-run.
+
+---
+
+## What the four symptoms mean
+
+| What you see | Most likely layer | Go to |
+|---|---|---|
+| Nothing at all — no error, no toast, bubble appears only for you | **Demo mode** | Step 0 |
+| Toast: "This conversation cannot be encrypted on this device yet" | **Peer key missing** | Step 5 |
+| Toast: "Message was not sent — secure sync failed" | **INSERT refused** | Steps 1–3 |
+| Bubble shows SENT, the other person gets nothing | **Realtime or decrypt** | Steps 4–5 |
+| Other person sees `[Encrypted message]` | **Key mismatch** | Step 5 |
+
+---
+
+## SQL that settles it in one query
+
+Paste with both user ids substituted. It answers Steps 1–3 at once.
+
+```sql
+with
+  a as (select '<USER_A_UUID>'::uuid as id),
+  b as (select '<USER_B_UUID>'::uuid as id),
+  shared as (
+    select cm.conversation_id
+    from public.conversation_members cm, a
+    where cm.user_id = a.id
+    intersect
+    select cm.conversation_id
+    from public.conversation_members cm, b
+    where cm.user_id = b.id
+  )
+select
+  (select count(*) from shared)                                          as shared_conversations,
+  (select count(*) from public.conversation_members cm
+     join shared s on s.conversation_id = cm.conversation_id)            as total_membership_rows,
+  (select bool_or(has_table_privilege('authenticated','public.messages','INSERT'))
+     from public.messages limit 1)                                       as messages_insert_granted,
+  (select count(*) from pg_policies
+     where schemaname='public' and tablename='messages'
+       and cmd='INSERT' and permissive='PERMISSIVE')                     as messages_permissive_insert_policies,
+  (select count(*) from pg_policies
+     where schemaname='public' and tablename='messages'
+       and cmd='SELECT' and permissive='PERMISSIVE')                     as messages_permissive_select_policies,
+  (select count(*) from pg_publication_tables
+     where pubname='supabase_realtime' and tablename='messages')         as messages_in_realtime_publication,
+  (select relreplident from pg_class where relname='messages')           as messages_replica_identity;
+```
+
+Expected: `shared_conversations` ≥ 1, `total_membership_rows` = 2,
+grants `true`, both policy counts ≥ 1, publication `1`, replica identity `f`.
+
+Any value that differs is the layer to fix. Send me the output and I will tell
+you exactly what to change.
