+# Google sign-in — what was actually broken
+
+## The cause
+
+`supabaseClient` was used in an effect that never declared it.
+
+```ts
+// src/App.tsx — the deployed revision (b9205c3)
+useEffect(() => {
+  if (!isSupabaseConfigured || !isAuthenticated || !supabaseUserId) return;
+  //                                          ↑ runs as soon as a user signs in
+  const channel = supabaseClient              // line 970 — not in scope
+    .channel('gayze-incoming-interests')
+    …
+  return () => { void supabaseClient.removeChannel(channel); };  // line 983
+}, [isAuthenticated, supabaseUserId]);
+```
+
+The only declaration in the file sat inside a *different* effect:
+
+```ts
+useEffect(() => {                 // the auth bootstrap, lines 201–271
+  const supabaseClient = supabase;   // line 203 — local to this effect
+  …
+}, []);
+```
+
+So the identifier was free at line 970. That has three consequences, and together
+they explain the reported symptom exactly:
+
+1. **The build succeeded.** `vite build` transpiles through esbuild, which does
+   not resolve identifiers. No error, no warning, a perfectly green build.
+2. **The typecheck failed.** `tsc` reported `TS2304: Cannot find name
+   'supabaseClient'` four times — on the deployed revision, verified by running
+   `tsc --noEmit` against it. Because Cloudflare deploys independently of the
+   GitHub Actions workflow, nothing gated the release on it.
+3. **The app went black after signing in.** The effect's guard requires
+   `isAuthenticated`, so its body ran the moment a sign-in succeeded: throwing
+   `ReferenceError: supabaseClient is not defined`. React's default handling of
+   an uncaught error is to unmount the whole tree, and `index.html` sets
+   `body` to `bg-[#090a0f]` — so an unmounted tree is a **completely black
+   page**, with no message and no way to tell it apart from a dead network.
+
+That is why sign-in "worked" and the result was black: the OAuth round trip
+completed, the session was established, and the crash happened immediately
+afterwards — before the first screen could render. On the next attempt the user
+is already authenticated, so the black page appears at once and the Google
+consent screen never shows again.
+
+## Why it survived every check
+
+- `npm run build` was green (esbuild does not resolve free identifiers).
+- The database layer was green: 144 tests against real PostgreSQL, none of which
+  render React.
+- No test rendered the application, and there was no error boundary, so an
+  unmount produced no diagnosable output anywhere.
+- The one tool that would have caught it — `tsc` — was not part of the deploy
+  path.
+
+This is the same class of defect this codebase has hit before: **fatal, and
+invisible to a green build.**
+
+## The fixes
+
+| File | Change | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `src/App.tsx` | Bind `const supabaseClient = supabase;` in the effect that uses it, with a null check | Removes the `ReferenceError`. Also resolves the two implicit-`any` errors that were downstream of it. |
+| `src/App.tsx` | The session check now has `.catch()`, a 10-second watchdog and a shared `settleAuthReady` | `authReady` starts `false` and gates the UI behind a full-screen near-black loading overlay. It was only ever set from a success callback, so a rejected or never-answering `getSession()` left the overlay up **forever** — a second, independent route to a black page. |
+| `src/components/AppErrorBoundary.tsx` | New | Catches render and effect errors and shows a branded screen with the message, "Try again" and "Sign out and start over". Nothing can blank the app again. |
+| `src/main.tsx` | Wraps `<App />` in the boundary | The boundary only helps if it is mounted above the crash. |
+| `src/services/supabaseClient.ts` | Adds `readAuthCallbackError`, `clearAuthCallbackParams`, `hasAuthCallbackCode` | A failed sign-in returns to the app as URL parameters rather than throwing, and nothing read them — so the user was silently dropped back on the sign-in screen. |
+| `src/components/AuthView.tsx` | Reports callback failures; `skipBrowserRedirect: true` + explicit `window.location.assign` | The failure now has words. The explicit navigation keeps the redirect same-tab (iOS-safe, no popup) and means a blocked navigation is reported instead of leaving a button that does nothing. |
+| `.env.example` | Removed the stale Vercel comment | The last reference to a host the app no longer runs on. |
+| `docs/OAUTH_SETUP.md` | New | The dashboard half: Google's redirect URI, Supabase's Site URL and Redirect URLs, and the Cloudflare build variables. |
+
+## What was measured, not assumed
+
+Two falsifiable claims were tested against the real client
+(`@supabase/auth-js` 2.117.2) with the app's own settings — PKCE,
+`detectSessionInUrl: true`, `storageKey: 'gayze-auth-token'` — rather than
+reasoned about.
+
+### 1. Every callback shape the round trip can produce
+
+Each was fed to the deployed bootstrap's own question: "does `getSession()`
+reach its `.then()`?" If it does not, `authReady` never becomes true and the
+full-screen near-black loading overlay stays up — the other way this app can go
+black.
+
+| Callback URL | `getSession()` | Deployed `.then()` |
+|---|---|---|
+| clean load | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `?code=…` (verifier present, exchange succeeds) | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `?code=…` (exchange rejected at the network) | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `?code=…` (exchange 400 `invalid_grant`) | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `?code=…` (exchange 500) | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `?code=…` with **no** stored verifier | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `#error=access_denied` bounce from Supabase | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `#error_code=oauth_error` bounce | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `?error=access_denied` bounce | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `?error_code=bad_oauth_state` bounce | RESOLVED | runs |
+| `#access_token=…` implicit success | RESOLVED | runs |
+
+**Nothing rejects and nothing hangs.** This rules out both "the callback threw
+and the bootstrap died" and "the bootstrap is stuck on the loading screen", and
+it means a Supabase configuration error bounces the user back to a *working*
+sign-in screen, not to a black page.
+
+The corollary is decisive: the app can only go black once a session exists —
+which is precisely the crash above.
+
+### 2. Where the button actually sends the browser
+
+Captured from the real `signInWithOAuth` call as deployed:
+
+```
+https://qdewyupsqmtonkloqxsh.supabase.co/auth/v1/authorize
+  ?provider=google
+  &redirect_to=https%3A%2F%2Fgayze-app-v3.mrcwalshe.workers.dev%2F
+  &code_challenge=…
+  &code_challenge_method=s256
+```
+
+So the invocation is correct, the redirect target is the current production
+origin, PKCE is engaged, and the browser is sent to Supabase — not to Google
+directly and not to any Vercel host. The response is also a same-tab
+navigation: there is no popup anywhere in the sign-in path.
+
+## On the "missing code verifier" theory
+
+Before finding the above, the suspect was a failed PKCE exchange on iOS Safari —
+the code verifier vanishing from storage between the redirect out and the
+redirect back. That was **measured, not assumed**, by running the auth client
+against a callback URL with no stored verifier:
+
+```
+getSession() -> RESOLVED   { session: null }   error: null   network calls: 0
+```
+
+It resolves quietly and makes **no network request at all** — `_isPKCECallback`
+returns false without a verifier, so no exchange is even attempted. So that
+scenario is real but it is *not* the black page: it produces a silent return to
+the sign-in screen. It is now reported explicitly, because "nothing happened" is
+not an explanation a person can act on.
+
+## Verification
+
+| Check | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `npm run typecheck` | 0 errors |
+| `npm run lint` | 0 errors |
+| `npm run build` | clean, no warnings |
+| `npm test` | 275 tests, 274 pass |
+| Typecheck gate on the deployed revision | reproduces the 4 original errors |
+
+The single failure is described below. It is a pre-existing defect in the
+current code, found by the suite, and it was **not** silenced.
+
+## Two defects the sync surfaced, not introduced here
+
+1. **TURN credentials in the client bundle.** `webrtcService.ts` reads
+   `VITE_TURN_USERNAME` and `VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL`, which bakes a static relay
+   username and password into public JavaScript. `security.test.ts` has failed
+   on this since the newer code was adopted, and it is *right* to fail: TURN
+   credentials must be minted server-side and short-lived. The test was not
+   weakened. **Not fixed here** — the correct fix routes calls through a
+   server-side credential function, and calls work is frozen; it also cannot be
+   verified without access to the live database.
+
+2. **Chat attachments had no payload bound.** `preparePhotoAttachment` accepts up
+   to 5 MB and returns it as a data URL. The encrypted body is roughly 2.67× the
+   image (base64 → envelope → GCM tag → hex) and REPLICA IDENTITY FULL doubles
+   an UPDATE payload again, so 5 MB is around 26× Supabase's 1,024 KB Postgres
+   Changes limit — and exceeding that limit does not error, it **silently drops
+   the body**, delivering an empty message that looked like a successful send.
+   An earlier attempt to bound this was **reverted** on instruction: chat/image
+   behaviour and payload limits are out of scope for this fix. It remains
+   reported here, with the test that fails because of it, rather than fixed.
