+# Google sign-in — configuration
+
+The code half of Google sign-in is in `AuthView` and `supabaseClient`. This is the
+half that lives in dashboards, and it is where the flow most often breaks,
+because a misconfigured redirect does not produce an error the app can see: the
+provider simply sends the browser somewhere wrong, or nowhere.
+
+Project reference (this is in the client bundle by design, and it is what makes
+the callback address below concrete): **`qdewyupsqmtonkloqxsh`**
+
+---
+
+## 1. The chain, and who owns each hop
+
+```
+GAYZE (Cloudflare)            Supabase                       Google
+──────────────────            ────────                       ──────
+taps "Continue with Google"
+  └─ signInWithOAuth ───────► /auth/v1/authorize?provider=google
+                                        └─────────────────►  consent screen
+                                                              │
+GAYZE callback  ◄──────────── /auth/v1/callback  ◄────────────┘
+  ?code=…                       (exchange)          redirect_uri =
+  (PKCE exchange)                                    https://qdewyupsqmtonkloqxsh.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
+```
+
+Two redirect addresses, owned by two different people:
+
+| Address | Configured in | Must be |
+|---|---|---|
+| `https://qdewyupsqmtonkloqxsh.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback` | Google Cloud console | **exactly this**, in *Authorised redirect URIs* |
+| The GAYZE origin the browser is on | Supabase dashboard | listed in *Redirect URLs* |
+
+The app sends the second one: `getAuthRedirectUrl()` returns
+`window.location.origin`, so it adapts to whichever host is serving the page.
+That is why nothing needs changing in code when the deployment moves — and why
+the dashboard must list **every** host that will ever serve the app.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Google Cloud console
+
+**APIs & Services → Credentials → your OAuth 2.0 Client ID** (Web application):
+
+- **Authorised JavaScript origins**
+
+  ```
+  https://gayze-app-v3.mrcwalshe.workers.dev
+  ```
+
+- **Authorised redirect URIs** — the Supabase callback, and **only** that:
+
+  ```
+  https://qdewyupsqmtonkloqxsh.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
+  ```
+
+  Do not add the Workers URL here. Google redirects to Supabase, and Supabase
+  redirects to the app; adding the app's own URL creates a second path that
+  bypasses the code exchange and produces exactly the "returned but not signed
+  in" symptom.
+
+The **Client ID** and **Client secret** go into Supabase, and nowhere else.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Supabase dashboard — Authentication → Providers → Google
+
+- Enabled, with the Client ID and Client secret from step 2.
+- Save.
+
+## 4. Supabase dashboard — Authentication → URL Configuration
+
+This is the most common cause of a sign-in that appears to do nothing.
+
+- **Site URL** — the canonical production origin:
+
+  ```
+  https://gayze-app-v3.mrcwalshe.workers.dev
+  ```
+
+  If this still points at an old Vercel or `localhost` address, that is where
+  sign-in will send people whenever the requested redirect is not on the list
+  below.
+
+- **Redirect URLs** — add every origin the app is served from:
+
+  ```
+  https://gayze-app-v3.mrcwalshe.workers.dev/**
+  https://gayze-app-v3.mrcwalshe.workers.dev/
+  http://localhost:3000/**
+  ```
+
+  Add any custom domain here too, and keep the `/**` entries: Supabase matches
+  the configured patterns against the `redirect_to` the app sends, and a pattern
+  that does not match is silently ignored in favour of the Site URL.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Cloudflare Workers environment variables
+
+The app reads its Supabase credentials at **build time**, so they must be
+available when the bundle is built, not merely at runtime:
+
+| Variable | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `VITE_SUPABASE_URL` | `https://qdewyupsqmtonkloqxsh.supabase.co` |
+| `VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` | the publishable (anon) key — **never** the service-role key |
+
+For a Workers Builds project, set these under **Settings → Variables and Secrets**
+for the **build** environment, then **redeploy**. Vite inlines them into the
+bundle, so changing a variable without a rebuild changes nothing.
+
+> `supabaseClient.ts` currently also carries a hard-coded fallback for both
+> values, which is why sign-in can work even when the variables are missing. It
+> exists to stop a build silently falling back to demo mode. Environment
+> variables still take precedence, so setting them is still the right thing to
+> do — the fallback should be treated as a safety net, not a configuration.
+
+---
+
+## 6. What was deliberately *not* changed
+
+- **PKCE stays on.** `flowType: 'pkce'` is untouched. That is the CSRF
+  protection for the callback; `signInWithOAuth` also keeps its `state`
+  round-trip. Nothing in this change disables nonce or state verification, and
+  nothing should: those checks are what stop an attacker replaying a callback.
+- **No popup.** The flow is a same-tab, full-page navigation via
+  `window.location.assign`, which is what iOS Safari requires. `window.open` is
+  not used anywhere in the sign-in path.
+- **No hard-coded host.** The redirect is built from `window.location.origin`
+  and a test asserts the result never contains `vercel.app`, `netlify` or
+  `workers.dev`.
+
+---
+
+## 7. How to tell which hop failed
+
+| What you see | Where the fault is |
+|---|---|
+| A black or blank page after tapping the button | **Fixed** — see `docs/OAUTH_FIX.md`. If it recurs, the error boundary failed to catch it; the browser console will now say why. |
+| Google shows *"Error 400: redirect_uri_mismatch"* | Step 2. The redirect URI does not match the Supabase callback exactly. |
+| Google signs in, then the app returns to the sign-in screen with a red message | The callback reached the app but the exchange failed. The message carries the provider's own reason. |
+| Google signs in, app returns, nothing happens at all | Step 4 — the `redirect_to` is not in the allow-list, so Supabase redirected to the Site URL instead. Supabase logs this under **Logs → Auth**. |
+| Signed in on the web but the app behaves as though it is not | The session is stored under `gayze-auth-token`; check the browser's localStorage for that key. |
