@@ -0,0 +1,299 @@
+# Preflight — what PASS and FAIL look like
+
+`docs/PREFLIGHT.sql` prints the state of the live Supabase project. This file
+says what the output must say for the deployment to proceed, section by section.
+
+**The live database has not been inspected from this repository and is
+UNVERIFIED.** There is no Supabase CLI, no `psql`, and no project credentials in
+the environment this work was done in. Nothing in this repository can be
+substituted for running the script; the harness reconstructs a base schema from
+the migrations, and a reconstruction cannot tell you what your project actually
+contains.
+
+Run it in the Supabase SQL editor. It is read-only: every statement is a
+`SELECT`, and a test asserts that none of them can alter the database.
+
+---
+
+## How to read this
+
+| Verdict | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| **PASS** | Exactly as expected. Continue. |
+| **FAIL** | Stop. This is a defect, and the section says what to do. |
+| **EXPECTED** | A value that is not `ok` but is correct for this project. |
+
+The distinction that matters throughout: a **missing permissive policy** and a
+**missing GRANT** both produce `permission denied`, but they need different
+fixes, and neither is visible from the client.
+
+---
+
+## Section 1 — are the migrations applied?
+
+**PASS:** every row reads `ok`.
+
+Six migrations, applied in filename order:
+
+| Migration | Creates |
+|---|---|
+| `20260928120000_profile_photos` | `profile_photos` |
+| `20260928130000_messaging_reliability` | `conversation_reads`, `messages.client_id`, `conversations.default_ttl_seconds` |
+| `20260928140000_call_signalling` | `call_signals` |
+| `20260928150000_timed_albums` | `album_items` |
+| `20260928160000_push_subscriptions` | `push_subscriptions` |
+| `20260928170000_immutable_lifetime` | three lifetime guards and `burn_message()` |
+
+**FAIL:** any row reads `MISSING`. That migration has not been applied. Apply the
+missing files in filename order — they are idempotent, so re-running an applied
+one is harmless.
+
+**FAIL:** a migration is applied but an earlier one is not. `supabase db push`
+applies in filename order; a gap usually means a file was run by hand. Apply the
+missing ones in order and re-run this section.
+
+---
+
+## Section 2 — the permissive/restrictive split on the message tables
+
+**This is the section that decides whether messaging can work at all, and it is
+the one to read first.**
+
+PostgreSQL combines policies as *(any PERMISSIVE matches)* **AND** *(all
+RESTRICTIVE match)*. A RESTRICTIVE policy can only ever narrow. So a table with
+RLS enabled and no PERMISSIVE policy for a command is denied for **everyone** —
+and no migration can grant that back, because a restrictive policy cannot widen.
+
+**PASS:** every `SELECT`/`INSERT` row for `messages` and `conversations` shows at
+least one permissive policy, and the `verdict` column reads `ok` throughout.
+
+**FAIL:** any row reads `DENIED FOR EVERYONE`. That feature cannot work.
+
+* If it is `messages` or `conversations`, `20260928130000` repairs it — its
+  guarded block creates the missing permissive policy, scoped to membership, and
+  never drops or replaces one that exists. The `notice` output of the apply names
+  exactly what it found and what it added. Read that output; do not assume.
+* If it is any **other** table, nothing repairs it automatically and nothing
+  should. A permissive policy has to be written deliberately, with the live
+  schema in front of you, because the correct predicate depends on what that
+  table is for. Do not copy the messages policy across.
+
+**A note on which policies are load-bearing.** Under the harness's reconstructed
+base schema, the guarded block adds `messages_sender_delete` and
+`conversations_members_update` and finds the rest already present. **Your project
+may differ**, which is precisely why this section exists. If the block reports
+creating something the harness did not, that is not an error — it means your base
+schema differs from the reconstruction, which is expected and is the thing this
+script is here to reveal.
+
+---
+
+## Section 3 — RLS and grants
+
+**PASS:** for every table listed:
+
+* `rls_enabled` is `true`;
+* `anon_select` is `false`;
+* `authenticated` has the privileges its features need (see the table below).
+
+**FAIL:** any table shows `RLS IS OFF`. Every row is readable by anyone holding
+the anon key. Enable it immediately:
+
+```sql
+alter table public.<table> enable row level security;
+```
+
+**FAIL:** `anon_select` is `true` on a table holding user content. Nothing in
+this application has an anonymous surface, so this is either a mistake or a
+policy written for a different project.
+
+**FAIL:** `app_secrets` shows `SECRETS READABLE`. It holds the TURN shared
+secret; no client role may hold any privilege on it.
+
+```sql
+revoke all on public.app_secrets from anon, authenticated;
+```
+
+**Expected privileges per table** — RLS decides *which rows*, a grant decides
+whether the table can be touched at all, and a missing grant fails before any
+policy is consulted:
+
+| Table | `authenticated` needs |
+|---|---|
+| `profiles`, `conversations`, `conversation_members` | `SELECT` |
+| `messages` | `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` |
+| `conversation_reads` | `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE` |
+| `calls` | `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE` |
+| `call_signals` | `SELECT`, `INSERT` |
+| `albums`, `album_items` | `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` |
+| `push_subscriptions` | `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` |
+| `profile_photos` | `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` |
+| `app_secrets` | **nothing** |
+
+**EXPECTED:** `messages` showing `UPDATE` and `DELETE` is correct and necessary.
+`UPDATE` is how a sender burns their own message; `DELETE` is how the purge
+removes expired rows. Both are constrained by restrictive policies to the sender.
+
+---
+
+## Section 4 — sequences
+
+**PASS:** `call_signals_id_seq` shows `auth_usage = true`.
+
+**FAIL:** `INSERT will fail for authenticated`. A table-level grant does **not**
+confer `USAGE` on the sequence behind a serial column, and that omission is
+invisible in review — the table looks granted. This was a real defect in an
+earlier revision of `20260928140000`.
+
+```sql
+grant usage, select on sequence public.call_signals_id_seq to authenticated;
+```
+
+---
+
+## Section 5 — SECURITY DEFINER functions
+
+Every function here runs with its owner's rights, so an unpinned `search_path`
+lets a caller shadow something it depends on, and a missing grant makes it
+silently uncallable — `service_role` is not a superuser in Supabase.
+
+**PASS:** every row shows `security_definer = true`, `pins_pg_temp = true`,
+`verdict = ok`, and a named `grantees` list that is not `(nobody)`.
+
+**FAIL:** `search_path is not pinned with pg_temp`. `pg_temp` must be pinned
+**last**: without it a temporary object can be created ahead of `public` in the
+search order even when `public` is named.
+
+**FAIL:** `nobody can call it — a missing grant`. A `revoke all ... from public`
+with no matching grant leaves the function reachable only by its owner. This is
+how the push recipient resolver, the push retirement helper and all three
+scheduled sweeps were uncallable — background notifications would have failed
+silently and forever.
+
+**EXPECTED:** `burn_message` lists `authenticated, service_role`. It is a client
+operation. Its privilege is deliberate: it needs to set `burned_at` on a row that
+RLS then hides, and a function is the only way to do that. It re-checks that the
+caller is the sender, and returns the same answer for "not yours" as for "does
+not exist".
+
+**EXPECTED:** `get_turn_credentials` lists `authenticated`. Clients need it to
+call.
+
+**EXPECTED:** the three purge/expire functions list `service_role` only.
+Cleanup is server-side; `authenticated` must not be able to invoke a sweep.
+
+---
+
+## Section 6 — TURN configuration
+
+**PASS:** both `turn_shared_secret` and `turn_urls` are listed as present.
+
+**FAIL / INCOMPLETE:** any `missing:` row. Without them `get_turn_credentials()`
+returns nothing, calls fall back to STUN-only, and they fail behind
+carrier-grade NAT — which is most mobile networks.
+
+```sql
+insert into public.app_secrets (key, value) values
+  ('turn_shared_secret', '<coturn static-auth-secret>'),
+  ('turn_urls', 'turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=udp,turns:turn.example.com:5349?transport=tcp,turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=tcp')
+on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();
+```
+
+Then **verify the HMAC**, which this script cannot do: the harness shims
+`pgcrypto`, so the credential's shape has been tested and the HMAC itself has
+not.
+
+```sql
+-- As service_role, or in the SQL editor.
+select * from public.get_turn_credentials(3600);
+-- Expect: urls, a username containing an expiry, and a credential.
+-- Confirm the credential validates against your coturn static-auth-secret.
+```
+
+---
+
+## Section 7 — Realtime
+
+**PASS:** every table shows `in_publication = true`, and `messages`, `albums` and
+`album_items` show `replica_identity = f` (FULL).
+
+**FAIL:** `NOT PUBLISHED — changes never reach the client`. The symptom is a
+message that appears only after a manual refresh, which is indistinguishable from
+a network problem.
+
+```sql
+alter publication supabase_realtime add table public.<table>;
+```
+
+**FAIL:** `REPLICA IDENTITY is not FULL` on `messages`, `albums` or
+`album_items`. Without it, an `UPDATE` or `DELETE` arrives without the previous
+row, so an expired or burned message cannot be removed from the UI — it stays on
+screen until something else refreshes the thread.
+
+```sql
+alter table public.messages replica identity full;
+```
+
+---
+
+## Section 8 — Storage
+
+**PASS:** `album-media` and `profile-photos` are `public = false`, and every
+policy in `storage.objects` is scoped by ownership.
+
+**FAIL:** `PUBLIC BUCKET`. A public bucket makes a signed URL meaningless —
+anyone with the path can read the object, before or after the album expires, and
+no application code can change that.
+
+**FAIL:** any policy on `storage.objects` whose predicate does not test the
+folder the object is in. The album policies derive ownership from the path
+(`{owner_id}/{album_id}/{item_id}.{ext}`), and a policy that only checks
+`bucket_id` is a public bucket by another name.
+
+**Note on signed URLs.** These cannot be revoked: once Storage has issued one, it
+is valid for the whole TTL it was minted with, whatever happens to the album.
+The client now caps the TTL by the album's remaining lifetime, so a signature
+never outlives its album — but that is a client-side guarantee. It holds only as
+long as the code that mints signatures is the code in this repository.
+
+---
+
+## Section 9 — scheduled jobs
+
+**PASS:** rows for `purge_expired_messages`, `purge_expired_albums` and
+`expire_stale_calls`, all `active`.
+
+**NOT A SECURITY FAILURE if absent.** Expiry is enforced by the read policies,
+evaluated at read time, so an expired row is unreadable whether or not a sweep
+has run. These jobs reclaim space and keep the tables small. Without them,
+expired rows accumulate.
+
+```sql
+select cron.schedule('gayze-purge-messages', '*/15 * * * *',
+  $$select public.purge_expired_messages(interval '1 hour')$$);
+select cron.schedule('gayze-purge-albums', '*/15 * * * *',
+  $$select public.purge_expired_albums(interval '1 hour')$$);
+select cron.schedule('gayze-expire-calls', '* * * * *',
+  $$select public.expire_stale_calls(interval '2 minutes', interval '30 seconds')$$);
+```
+
+---
+
+## What this script cannot tell you
+
+Stated plainly, because a PASS here is not the same as a verified deployment.
+
+1. **Storage API behaviour.** Signed-URL expiry, and whether a URL issued before
+   expiry fails after it, is enforced by Storage itself. Section 8 shows the
+   policies that are the precondition — no signature can be minted for an object
+   that is not selectable — but the API is not exercised.
+2. **The HMAC in `get_turn_credentials()`.** Untested here; see section 6.
+3. **Anything requiring a browser or a phone.** Autoplay, backgrounded ringing,
+   Wi-Fi-to-cellular handover, and push timing on a terminated app. Desktop
+   Chrome is not evidence for any iOS behaviour.
+4. **Whether a push actually arrives.** Section 5 proves the sender can resolve
+   recipients and retire dead endpoints. Only a device proves delivery.
+5. **Your base schema's permissive policies**, beyond the summary in section 2.
+   If a permissive policy on the live `messages` table is broader than the one
+   the harness reconstructs, RLS is weaker than the test suite suggests, and
+   section 2 is the closest this script comes to revealing that.
