+# GAYZE — Production Validation Report
+
+**Date:** 29 September 2026
+**Scope:** messaging reliability, audio/video calling, server-enforced expiry, timed albums, secure media, notifications, iOS/mobile resilience, secret exposure.
+**Method:** every migration applied to real PostgreSQL and attacked; every claim below marked *executed* was run in this environment and is reproducible with `npm test`.
+
+> **This report does not say the system is production ready.** It says precisely
+> which parts were verified, by what evidence, and which parts could not be
+> verified here and therefore require the operator. Three of the defects found
+> were fatal — album creation, push delivery, and notification sound on iPhone —
+> and all three were invisible to reading, to typechecking and to a green build.
+> That is the argument for the harness, not a claim about the product.
+
+---
+
+## 1. VERIFIED
+
+Each item below was executed. Nothing in this section is an inference.
+
+### 1.1 Every migration applies to real PostgreSQL, and applies twice
+
+`tests/harness.ts` builds a Supabase-shaped database — the `anon`,
+`authenticated` and `service_role` roles, `auth.uid()` read from
+`request.jwt.claim.sub`, the `storage` schema, `pgcrypto` shims — reconstructs
+the base schema, then applies **every migration in `supabase/migrations/` in
+filename order, exactly as `supabase db push` would**.
+
+Verified by execution:
+
+* all five migrations apply in order;
+* the complete set applies a **second** time without error;
+* no duplicate policies accumulate on the second pass;
+* `grep -c polname` over the test suite is 0 — `pg_policies` exposes
+  `policyname`/`qual`/`with_check`, and the earlier field is not real;
+* every constraint that must survive a re-run is added by an explicit
+  `alter table … add constraint`, because `create table if not exists` silently
+  skips the constraints declared inside it on a re-run — leaving a re-applied
+  database weaker than a fresh one.
+
+### 1.2 The adversarial matrix is denied at the database layer
+
+`tests/security.rls.test.ts`, 53 tests. Every case asserts denial **in
+PostgreSQL**, never in React:
+
+| Attack | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Read another member's messages | denied |
+| Insert into a conversation you do not belong to | denied |
+| Forge `sender_id` to another user | denied |
+| Read another call's signalling | denied |
+| Inject SDP into another call | denied |
+| Read an expired message (as author, as recipient) | invisible to both |
+| Read an expired album | invisible |
+| Retrieve another member's album media | denied |
+| Store an album item at a well-formed path in someone else's folder | denied by CHECK |
+| Reuse a signed URL after expiry | object is not selectable, so no URL can be minted |
+| Manipulate `conversation_id` / `user_id` | denied |
+| `anon` accessing anything | denied everywhere |
+
+Supporting facts established by the same suite: `app_secrets` has **no grant to
+any role**; expired and burned messages are invisible to author *and* recipient;
+expiry is evaluated at read time rather than by a stored flag; no permissive
+policy widens `messages`, `conversations` or `storage.objects` beyond the
+guarded, membership-scoped set.
+
+### 1.3 Schema invariants
+
+Confirmed against the applied database, not against the file text:
+
+* every `SECURITY DEFINER` function pins `search_path` **and** `pg_temp`;
+* all 19 revoked functions have a grantee — none is owner-only;
+* sequence privileges are present (`call_signals_id_seq`), which a table-level
+  grant does not confer;
+* foreign-key delete rules are cascade or set-null, never orphan;
+* `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` is set on `messages`, `albums`, `album_items`;
+* the Realtime publication contains every subscribed table;
+* all ten CHECK constraints are present;
+* TTL values 0, 10, 60, 300, 3600, 86400, 604800 are accepted and 999 is
+  rejected;
+* indexes exist for the per-conversation read, the one-live-call partial unique
+  constraint, and the unread-count scan;
+* no migration contains `disable row level security`;
+* no migration contains a service-role key;
+* `get_turn_credentials()` derives its credential with `hmac(` — it never echoes
+  the stored secret.
+
+### 1.4 The push delivery path
+
+`tests/webpush.crypto.test.ts`, 8 tests, exercising the exact code production
+runs (extracted to `supabase/functions/_shared/webpush.ts` so Node can import
+it):
+
+* the VAPID key envelope is accepted by Web Crypto;
+* the signed JWT **verifies against its public key**, and its lifetime is within
+  the 24-hour limit the push services enforce;
+* a full RFC 8291 round trip, with the decryptor written separately from the
+  specification so a shared mistake cannot cancel itself out;
+* salt and ephemeral key are fresh on every message — nonce reuse in AES-GCM is
+  catastrophic, not merely untidy;
+* a tampered tag fails closed;
+* a payload encrypted for one device is unreadable by another;
+* the payload carries a display name and a conversation id and **no message
+  content, ciphertext or nonce**.
+
+### 1.5 Build and serving
+
+* `npm run typecheck` — clean
+* `npm run lint` — clean
+* `npm run build` — clean
+* `npm test` — **152 tests, 7 files, all passing; 101 of them execute real SQL**
+
+Production output inspected, all 30 files, 1.57 M characters of text assets:
+
+* no `service_role` key, no `sb_secret_`, no JWT, no `VAPID_PRIVATE`;
+* no `VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL`, `VITE_TURN_SECRET` or `TURN_STATIC_AUTH_SECRET`
+  anywhere in the repository **or the built output**;
+* no `VITE_` variable name of any kind reaches the bundle;
+* the only `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `turn:` and `console.log` matches are
+  inside vendored library internals (`supabase-js`, `zxing`) or the deliberate
+  decoy screen — each examined individually and none is a leak;
+* one service worker, `public/gayze-sw.js`, registered once with
+  `updateViaCache: 'none'`;
+* `manifest.webmanifest` served as `application/manifest+json`, `gayze-sw.js` at
+  the repo root so its scope is `/`, icons 200.
+
+---
+
+## 2. FIXED
+
+Ordered by severity. The first three were fatal in production and were invisible
+to reading, to typechecking, and to a green build.
+
+### 2.1 Album creation was impossible for every user — **fatal**
+
+`albums`' SELECT policy called `gayze_can_view_album()`, a STABLE function.
+
+A STABLE function is evaluated against the **statement snapshot**, which does
+not contain the row the current `INSERT` is creating — and `INSERT … RETURNING`
+requires the SELECT policy to pass. So `insert into albums (…) returning *`
+failed with *"new row violates row-level security policy"* for owners and
+members alike. The album feature could not create an album.
+
+Proven by probe before the fix: `insert` **without** `RETURNING` succeeded,
+`insert … returning id` was denied, and a stable subquery saw 0 albums.
+
+Fixed by rewriting the policy as row expressions. `gayze_can_view_album()`
+remains the authority for Storage only, and a drift test asserts the two agree
+across a matrix of owners, visibilities and expiry states.
+
+> **The general lesson, and the most important one in this phase:** never build a
+> SELECT policy on a STABLE function if `INSERT … RETURNING` must work.
+
+### 2.2 Every push notification would have been rejected — **fatal**
+
+The PKCS#8 envelope built around the raw P-256 private scalar was malformed:
+36 bytes under a header declaring 35. A stray `0x08` preceded the second OID and
+its tag was written as `0x08` instead of `0x06`.
+
+Web Crypto rejected the key outright, so no Authorization header was ever
+produced and **every push would have failed with a 401** from Apple, Google and
+Mozilla — the entire delivery path, silently, for every user.
+
+Nothing in the repository could have caught it: the function runs in Deno, its
+source is excluded from `tsconfig`, and no test touched it. It was found by
+extracting the crypto so it could be imported and tested. The signature now
+verifies.
+
+### 2.3 Notification sound was silent on every iPhone — **fatal on iOS**
+
+`notifySound()` constructed a **new** `AudioContext` per notification. iOS will
+not start an AudioContext outside a user gesture, and one created later is born
+suspended — so every chime was dropped.
+
+Fixed with a single shared context, primed on the first pointer/key/touch event
+of the session and resumed on every foreground return. The context is no longer
+closed after playing, because a closed context cannot be resumed.
+
+### 2.4 Three WebRTC reconnect defects
+
+* **TURN was never used on the first call of a session.** `setupPeerConnection()`
+  was fed `getIceServers()`, which returns the STUN-only fallback until
+  `fetchIceServers()` resolves. ICE gathering had already begun with no relay —
+  for the call that most needed it. Now resolved **before** the connection is
+  built.
+* **Signals were not drained while recovering.** The drain stops at `CONNECTED`,
+  and was not restarted on `disconnected`/`failed` — so an ICE restart's offer
+  was never read by the other side. Recovery now restarts the drain.
+* **A fast accept could be missed entirely.** `calls.state` arrives through
+  Realtime, but a subscriber that has not finished joining receives nothing, and
+  the `UPDATE` carrying "accepted" is a single event with no retry — the caller
+  would then never create an offer and the call would sit until the ring
+  timeout. The call row is now also polled on the existing 2.5 s drain tick.
+
+### 2.5 Restrictive policies could not restore access they narrowed
+
+Every policy this repository adds to `messages` and `conversations` is
+RESTRICTIVE, which only ever narrows. Combined with a table that has RLS and no
+permissive policy for a command, the result is that the feature stays broken —
+most importantly the disappearing-message timer, whose column the migration adds
+but whose write would have been denied.
+
+A guarded block now creates a permissive policy **only when none exists**, scoped
+to membership, never dropping or replacing anything, and a `notice` reports what
+it found and what it added. Two tests pin the property: a no-op against a schema
+that already has policies, a repair against one that has none.
+
+### 2.6 Five server-side functions were uncallable
+
+`push_targets_for_message`, `disable_push_subscription`, `purge_expired_messages`,
+`purge_expired_albums` and `expire_stale_calls` were each
+`revoke all … from public` with **no matching grant**. `service_role` is not a
+superuser in Supabase, so the push sender could not resolve recipients and could
+not retire a dead endpoint: background notifications would have failed silently,
+forever.
+
+### 2.7 Seven new tables had no GRANT at all
+
+Relying on project-level default privileges means a migration works in one
+project and fails closed in another. Each table now states the privileges its
+feature needs. `anon` is granted nothing anywhere — no anonymous messaging
+surface exists, and the absent grant is a second barrier behind RLS.
+
+### 2.8 Push subscription leaked across accounts on a shared device
+
+A push endpoint belongs to the browser profile, not the account. When a second
+person signed in on the same device, the endpoint stayed bound to the first —
+whose messages would then keep arriving on the new person's device.
+
+`register_push_subscription` is now SECURITY DEFINER and claims the endpoint
+exclusively. It takes no user id, so it cannot be used to subscribe someone else.
+Two tests cover both directions.
+
+### 2.9 The UI claimed DELIVERED for messages nobody confirmed
+
+Every own message read back from the server rendered `DELIVERED`, which asserts
+the recipient's device received it. Nothing in the architecture reports that:
+no receipt table, no acknowledgement signal, and `conversation_reads` is a
+per-conversation watermark the sender cannot see.
+
+Own messages now render `SENT` — the server holds the row, which is all that is
+known. `DELIVERED` and `READ` were deleted from the `MessageDeliveryState` union
+so the compiler refuses to let either return without the machinery behind it;
+it flagged both call sites immediately.
+
+### 2.10 Messaging correctness
+
+* **Duplicate bubbles.** The optimistic bubble and the Realtime echo could both
+  survive: if the INSERT arrived before the write promise resolved, renaming the
+  optimistic bubble left two with the same id. Now the optimistic bubble is
+  dropped when the stored row has already landed.
+* **First message of a new conversation was dropped.** A row for a conversation
+  not yet loaded caused a list refresh and was then discarded, so the thread
+  showed empty with no unread count. Rows are now buffered (bounded at 50) and
+  replayed once the refresh lands.
+* **A frontend deployed ahead of its migration took messaging down.** Without
+  `messages.client_id`, every send failed with a schema error. The client now
+  detects the missing column, retries without the idempotency key, and says so
+  loudly — trading retry-deduplication for a working feature.
+
+### 2.11 Schema and hygiene
+
+* `get_active_call()` returned **one row of nulls** instead of zero rows; changed
+  to `returns setof` (with the required `drop function`, since a return type
+  cannot be replaced in place).
+* Album item storage paths are now **derivable** from the row: segment one must
+  equal `owner_id`, segment two must equal `album_id`. Previously the shape CHECK
+  accepted any well-formed uuid triple, so a member could store an item pointing
+  into someone else's folder — never readable through it, but a misleading
+  dangling row that should not be storable.
+* `20260928120000_profile_photos.sql` had no rollback section; it has one now.
+
+---
+
+## 3. REQUIRES SUPABASE ACTION
+
+None of the following could be performed from this environment: there is no
+Supabase CLI, no `psql`, and no project credentials here. **The production
+database has not been migrated, and this report does not claim it has.**
+
+### 3.1 Run the preflight first
+
+`docs/PREFLIGHT.sql` — twelve read-only queries, no DDL, verified to change
+nothing (a test asserts every statement begins with `SELECT` and executes all of
+them against a migrated database).
+
+**Read section 2 of its output before applying anything.**
+
+The base schema of the production project is not in this repository; these
+migrations add to tables created outside it. PostgreSQL combines policies as
+*(any PERMISSIVE matches) **AND** (all RESTRICTIVE match)*, and a RESTRICTIVE
+policy can only ever narrow. So if `messages` has no permissive policy for a
+command, that command is denied for everyone and no migration can bring it back.
+
+Section 2 prints that per table and per command, and marks every case that
+cannot work. The migration's guarded block repairs it for `messages` and
+`conversations` only — any other table needs a permissive policy written
+deliberately, with the live schema in front of you.
+
+The harness reconstructs the base schema; it does not read the live project. It
+can therefore verify what this repository changes, but it **cannot certify the
+live policies**. This query is how that gap closes, and closing it requires you.
+
+### 3.2 Apply the migrations, in order
+
+```
+supabase db push
+```
+
+Or run each file in `supabase/migrations/` in filename order through the SQL
+editor. They are idempotent — verified by applying the complete set twice. Watch
+the `notice` output: the messaging migration reports which policies it found and
+which it created.
+
+**Any edit to a migration invalidates every test result above it.** Re-run
+`npm test`, then re-run the preflight and compare.
+
+### 3.3 Configure TURN
+
+Without this, calls work on direct and STUN-assisted paths only, and fail behind
+carrier-grade NAT — which is most mobile networks.
+
+```sql
+insert into public.app_secrets (key, value) values
+  ('turn_shared_secret', '<coturn static-auth-secret>'),
+  ('turn_urls', 'turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=udp,turns:turn.example.com:5349?transport=tcp,turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=tcp')
+on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();
+```
+
+### 3.4 Verify `get_turn_credentials()` on a real project
+
+It depends on `pgcrypto`'s `hmac()` and `gen_random_bytes()`, which PGlite does
+not ship. The harness shims them, so the **shape** of the result is tested but
+the HMAC itself is not. Confirm with a `service_role` client that it returns a
+username, a credential and URLs, and that the credential validates against your
+coturn `static-auth-secret`.
+
+### 3.5 Enable `pg_cron` and schedule cleanup
+
+The expiry policies are the security boundary, so nothing here is load-bearing
+for access control — these jobs reclaim space. The migrations schedule them when
+`pg_cron` is present and emit a `notice` when it is not:
+
+* `purge_expired_messages()`
+* `purge_expired_albums()` — also returns the storage paths it orphaned
+* `expire_stale_calls()`
+
+### 3.6 Deploy the push function and wire the webhook
+
+```
+supabase functions deploy send-push --no-verify-jwt
+supabase secrets set VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=... VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=... VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:hello@gayze.app
+```
+
+Generate the key pair with `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`. Then add a
+Database Webhook on `public.messages` (INSERT) pointing at `send-push`.
+
+The function treats its input as untrusted: it accepts a message id only,
+resolves recipients through `push_targets_for_message` under `service_role`, and
+returns `{delivered:0, reason}` when secrets are missing rather than failing
+open. A 404 or 410 from the push service disables that subscription.
+
+### 3.7 Album storage cleanup
+
+Postgres cannot delete a bucket object. `purge_expired_albums()` deletes rows and
+returns the storage paths; wire a scheduled Edge Function to remove them:
+
+```ts
+const { data } = await supabase.rpc('purge_expired_albums', { p_grace: '1 hour' });
+const paths = (data ?? []).map((row) => row.storage_path);
+if (paths.length) await supabase.storage.from('album-media').remove(paths);
+```
+
+### 3.8 Confirm bucket privacy
+
+Verify `album-media` and `profile-photos` are **private**. A public bucket makes
+a signed URL worthless: anyone with the path can read the object regardless of
+expiry. The preflight reports this.
+
+---
+
+## 4. REQUIRES ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
+
+The client reads exactly three variables, confirmed by grep:
+
+| Variable | Where | Required | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `VITE_SUPABASE_URL` | frontend build | yes | existing |
+| `VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` | frontend build | yes | existing; publishable by design |
+| `VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` | frontend build | for push | **public**, safe in the bundle |
+
+Edge Function secrets: `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`,
+`VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`, `VAPID_SUBJECT`.
+
+The build scan confirmed the VAPID public key was **absent** from the current
+output — it is not set in this environment, so push cannot activate until it is
+set at build time and redeployed. This is expected, not a defect.
+
+**No `VITE_TURN_*` variable exists anywhere**, by design. TURN credentials are
+minted server-side by `get_turn_credentials()` with a short TTL. There is no
+long-lived TURN secret in client source or in the built output.
+
+---
+
+## 5. REQUIRES REAL DEVICE TEST
+
+**Desktop Chrome is not evidence for any of these.** No browser and no mobile
+device exist in this environment, so nothing below has been observed and nothing
+below is claimed.
+
+### 5.1 iOS push with the app fully terminated
+
+Requires iOS 16.4+, and the user must have **added the site to the Home Screen**
+— Safari will not deliver Web Push to a tab. Confirm the subscription is created
+after the Home Screen install, not before.
+
+Then test: force-quit the app completely, have a second account send a message,
+and record whether and **when** the notification arrives. Apple does not
+guarantee background delivery, so timing is not ours to promise; record what
+actually happens rather than what the architecture intends.
+
+### 5.2 WebRTC
+
+* Wi-Fi → cellular handover mid-call (ICE restart; ≤3 attempts, caller re-offers)
+* screen lock and return
+* backgrounding to another app and returning
+* `CONNECTED` reflecting real connection state — never merely an offer/answer
+* `connecting` / `ringing` / `connected` / `reconnecting` / `ended` / `failed`
+  consistent on **both** peers
+* cancel, reject, ring timeout, terminate
+* `getUserMedia` cleanup: the camera/mic indicator must go dark after every
+  ending, including a crash of the other peer
+
+### 5.3 Messaging notifications
+
+The six receiver states, each of which needs a device: same conversation, other
+conversation, other screen, backgrounded, temporarily disconnected, reconnecting.
+For each: appears, unread increments, toast where appropriate, **no duplicate
+toast**, no notification for an own message, opening clears unread, reconnect
+does not replay hundreds of old notifications, subscription not duplicated.
+
+### 5.4 Autoplay and audio
+
+iOS blocks audio until a gesture. Confirm the first interaction primes the shared
+AudioContext and that a notification chime is audible afterwards — and that it
+stays silent rather than firing at an unrelated moment if it is not.
+
+### 5.5 Camera, microphone and notifications permissions
+
+Denial, grant, and revocation-while-running. A revoked permission must surface as
+a real error, not a silent dead call.
+
+---
+
+## 6. KNOWN PLATFORM LIMITATIONS
+
+These are properties of the platforms, not defects to fix. They are documented so
+nobody assumes otherwise.
+
+1. **iOS push requires iOS 16.4+, and the site added to the Home Screen.** In a
+   Safari tab there is no push host at all: Web Push cannot reach it, and no
+   amount of server work changes that. Users on older iOS will not receive
+   background notifications.
+2. **Background delivery is at Apple's discretion.** A push to a fully terminated
+   app may be delayed or coalesced with others. The architecture is correct; the
+   timing is not ours. Any UI implying instant delivery would be lying.
+3. **WebRTC calls cannot run while the app is backgrounded on iOS.** The browser
+   suspends the page; the call ends or degrades. `pagehide` with
+   `event.persisted` keeps the call alive across a bfcache restore, which is the
+   most that is possible.
+4. **TURN is required behind carrier-grade NAT.** Without the `app_secrets` rows
+   in §3.3, calls fail on many mobile networks. The UI names the missing relay
+   rather than blaming the user's connection.
+5. **Chat images travel as data URLs inside the encrypted envelope.** This works
+   and is end-to-end encrypted, but it inflates each message and is capped by the
+   message size limit. Moving to Storage with encryption is the natural next
+   step and is not done here — see §8.
+6. **`DELIVERED` cannot be honestly implemented under end-to-end encryption.**
+   The server cannot know a device decrypted a message, only that it was fetched.
+   It should stay unimplemented rather than approximated.
+
+---
+
+## 7. SECURITY FINDINGS
+
+### 7.1 Fatal, found by execution
+
+| # | Finding | Consequence | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `albums` SELECT policy on a STABLE function | `INSERT … RETURNING` denied for **every** user — album creation impossible | Fixed |
+| 2 | Malformed PKCS#8 VAPID envelope | **Every** push rejected with 401 by all push services | Fixed |
+| 3 | Five functions with `revoke` and no grant | Push recipient resolution and endpoint retirement impossible; cleanup jobs unable to run | Fixed |
+| 4 | Seven tables with no `GRANT` | Feature-dependent permission failures; fails closed in some projects, works in others | Fixed |
+| 5 | Restrictive policy with no permissive counterpart | Timer write denied; the feature the migration exists to fix stays broken | Fixed, guarded |
+
+### 7.2 Privacy and integrity
+
+| # | Finding | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| 6 | Push endpoint inherited across accounts on a shared device — one person's messages delivered to another's phone | Fixed |
+| 7 | UI claimed `DELIVERED` with no delivery evidence | Fixed |
+| 8 | Album item path not derivable from the row — a member could store a path into another's folder | Fixed |
+| 9 | `get_active_call()` returned a null row rather than none | Fixed |
+
+### 7.3 Confirmed as *not* vulnerable
+
+* No secret in the bundle: no service-role key, no `sb_secret_`, no long-lived
+  TURN credential, no VAPID private key — checked across all 30 build artifacts.
+* `app_secrets` is ungranted to every client role.
+* TURN credentials are short-lived and HMAC-derived; the stored secret is never
+  echoed to the client.
+* No migration disables RLS.
+* `anon` is denied everywhere.
+* Notification payloads carry no message content.
+* Expired content is inaccessible **server-side**, not merely hidden — verified
+  for messages, albums and album objects, as author and as recipient.
+* Expiry is enforced at read time from `expires_at`, not by a client timer.
+
+### 7.4 Residual risk
+
+* **The live permissive policies are unverified.** They are not in this
+  repository, and the harness cannot read them. If a permissive policy on the
+  live `messages` table is broader than the one reconstructed here, RLS is weaker
+  than these tests suggest. **§3.1 is the mitigation and it requires you.**
+* **`get_turn_credentials()`'s HMAC is untested here** (PGlite ships no
+  `pgcrypto`). Verify per §3.4.
+* **Storage API signed-URL expiry is enforced by Supabase Storage, not by this
+  code.** The harness proves an expired object is not selectable — the
+  precondition for no URL being mintable — but the Storage API itself is not
+  exercised. Confirm on a live project that a URL issued before expiry fails
+  after it.
+
+---
+
+## 8. REMAINING BUGS
+
+Honest list. None of these is a security hole; all are unfinished work.
+
+1. **Chat images as data URLs.** Works, end-to-end encrypted, but not a storage
+   design. Deciding whether to move to Storage with encryption and access control
+   is a product decision that has not been made.
+2. **Read receipts are not implemented.** The UI no longer claims them, and the
+   full specification is written in `docs/COMMUNICATIONS_LAYER.md`: expose other
+   members' `last_read_at` through a policy scoped to the caller's conversations,
+   derive `READ` when the minimum watermark passes the message's `created_at`,
+   and propagate over Realtime.
+3. **Group calling is not exposed.** The signalling tables are
+   conversation-scoped and support it; the UI is one-to-one only.
+4. **360 KB of unreferenced assets ship to every user:** `public/ChatGPT Image
+   Sep 27, 2026, 02_03_19 PM.png` (356 KB), `gayze-logo.webp`,
+   `gayze-official-logo.webp`. None is referenced anywhere in source, the
+   manifest or the service worker. They are the user's files and were therefore
+   reported rather than deleted — remove with
+   `git rm "public/ChatGPT Image Sep 27, 2026, 02_03_19 PM.png" public/gayze-logo.webp public/gayze-official-logo.webp`
+   if they are not wanted.
+5. **`supabase/functions` is excluded from `tsconfig`.** The send path is only
+   typechecked incidentally, because a test now imports it. That is fragile —
+   the exclusion should be narrowed to the entry point so the shared module stays
+   covered.
+6. **No browser test exists at all.** The database layer is genuinely tested; the
+   UI is not, and everything in §5 depends on that gap being closed by hand.
+
+---
+
+## 9. DEPLOYMENT STEPS
+
+Sequenced, with the reason each ordering matters.
+
+1. **Run `docs/PREFLIGHT.sql`.** Read section 2 first. Do not proceed past a row
+   reading `DENIED FOR EVERYONE` that you do not understand — those are features
+   that cannot work, and for anything outside `messages`/`conversations` the
+   migration will not repair them.
+2. **Confirm bucket privacy** (`album-media`, `profile-photos` must be private).
+   A public bucket defeats signed-URL expiry.
+3. **Apply the migrations in filename order** (`supabase db push`). Watch the
+   `notice` output. They are idempotent; applying twice is safe and creates no
+   duplicate policies.
+4. **Verify:** re-run the preflight. Sections 3–5 should read `ok` throughout.
+5. **Insert the TURN `app_secrets` rows** (§3.3), then **verify
+   `get_turn_credentials()`** with a `service_role` client (§3.4).
+6. **Enable `pg_cron`** and confirm the three cleanup jobs are scheduled (§3.5).
+7. **Deploy `send-push`** and set its five secrets (§3.6). Add the Database
+   Webhook on `public.messages` (INSERT).
+8. **Set `VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and rebuild the frontend.** The current build has
+   no public key, so push cannot activate without this.
+9. **Wire the album storage cleanup function** (§3.7).
+10. **Serve the PWA files.** `manifest.webmanifest`, `gayze-sw.js` and the icons
+    must be at the site root; `gayze-sw.js` at `/gayze-sw.js` so its scope is `/`.
+    Vite copies `public/` automatically — confirm on the deployed origin.
+11. **On a real iPhone:** add to Home Screen, then run §5.1 and §5.2.
+12. **Re-run `npm test` after touching any migration**, and re-run the preflight.
+
+---
+
+## 10. FINAL TEST CHECKLIST
+
+### Automated — run now, must pass
+
+```
+npm run typecheck     # clean
+npm run lint          # clean
+npm run build         # clean
+npm test              # 152 tests, 7 files, all passing
+```
+
+Expected: 101 tests execute real SQL against real PostgreSQL 18.3.
+
+| Suite | Tests | Covers |
+|---|---|---|
+| `tests/security.rls.test.ts` | 53 | Phase 3 adversarial matrix, at the database layer |
+| `tests/migrations.integrity.test.ts` | 48 | Phase 2 checklist, plus `docs/PREFLIGHT.sql` executed in full |
+| `tests/webpush.crypto.test.ts` | 8 | RFC 8291 round trip, VAPID signature verification |
+| `security.test.ts` | 16 | notification policy and secret-exposure guards |
+| `albumService.test.ts` / `notificationService.test.ts` / `realtimeManager.test.ts` | 27 | album file validation, notification dedupe, channel ref-counting |
+
+**This checklist tests the repository, not the deployment.** A green run here
+does not mean the live database is correct — see §3.1.
+
+### Against the live project — manual, required
+
+- [ ] `docs/PREFLIGHT.sql` section 2 read, every `DENIED FOR EVERYONE` understood
+- [ ] Preflight sections 3–5 read `ok` throughout
+- [ ] Buckets private; signed URL issued pre-expiry fails post-expiry
+- [ ] `get_turn_credentials()` returns a valid HMAC credential
+- [ ] Cleanup jobs scheduled; `purge_expired_albums()` paths wired to Storage removal
+- [ ] `send-push` deployed; webhook fires on INSERT
+- [ ] `VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` present in the deployed bundle
+- [ ] Expired message unreadable via a direct API call, as author and recipient
+- [ ] Expired album unreadable via a direct API call
+- [ ] Another member's media unreachable by path manipulation
+
+### On a real device — manual, required
+
+- [ ] iOS 16.4+, added to Home Screen, subscription created after install
+- [ ] Push received with the app force-quit; timing recorded honestly
+- [ ] Wi-Fi → cellular mid-call; ICE restart recovers
+- [ ] Screen lock and return; backgrounding and return
+- [ ] `CONNECTED` reflects real connection state on both peers
+- [ ] Cancel / reject / ring timeout / terminate all end cleanly on both peers
+- [ ] Camera and mic released after every ending
+- [ ] Six receiver states produce correct, non-duplicated notification behaviour
+- [ ] No notification for an own message; opening clears unread
+- [ ] Reconnect does not replay old notifications
+- [ ] Chime audible after first interaction; silent (not misfiring) if not
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+Three defects were fatal in production and invisible to reading, to
+typechecking and to a green build: **album creation was impossible for every
+user**, **every push notification would have been rejected with a 401**, and
+**notification sound was silent on every iPhone**. Two more — five uncallable
+server-side functions, and seven tables with no `GRANT` — would have produced
+silent, permanent failures in the notification and cleanup paths. All are fixed
+and pinned by tests.
+
+The database layer is now genuinely tested rather than assumed: 101 tests
+executing real SQL, including the full Phase 3 adversarial matrix and the
+migration set applied twice.
+
+What remains unverified, and is not claimed: **the live database** (its base
+schema is not in this repository, so a preflight script and an operator are
+required — §3.1), **anything requiring a browser**, and **anything requiring a
+real iOS or Android device** (§5). Desktop Chrome was not used as evidence for
+any iOS behaviour.
+
+The system should not be described as production ready until the preflight has
+been read against the live project and the device checklist in §10 has actually
+been run.
